From 9a161a928e5a35c5ceffa6ee1c50e51737f088af Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 26 Jul 2026 15:34:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Let people edit the storyboard before a topic video is
 rendered
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Topic videos with generated visuals (character scenes or AI b-roll) used to
run straight through: you typed a topic and waited several minutes to find
out whether the shots the model imagined were the shots you wanted. If one
scene missed, the whole video was wasted.

Those jobs now pause halfway. The cheap half runs first — write the script,
voice the narration, then generate one still per scene — and the job parks
in a new "awaiting_review" status with an editable plan on the row. The
studio shows every shot with its still, its narration line and its length;
you reword any prompt, redraw any still, then hit render. The expensive
half (image-to-video, or the Ken Burns encode) only runs after that.

Reviewing costs nothing extra. The stills on the plan ARE the frames the
render animates, so approving generates no image twice, and prompt edits and
redraws are unbilled (redraws are capped at two per scene so it is not an
open tap on image generation). Funding is still reserved when the job is
created; discarding a plan, or letting it expire unreviewed after a day,
gives the reservation back.

Scene lengths are read-only here, because they come from narration that has
already been recorded — changing one would desync every later scene from the
audio. The engines whose timelines are actually free get editable lengths in
the next patch.

Also adds a periodic sweep for video jobs that can never settle themselves:
unapproved storyboards past their window, and jobs orphaned in
queued/processing by a restart. The second case was a pre-existing money bug
— a restart left the row "processing" forever with the reservation spent.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
